nization, and persists in a variety of printed, sung, carved, painted, written, spoken, and digital media. From oral traditions transmitted through memory and mnemonics to contemporary genres and media, Native North American authors offer Indigenous perspectives on social, political, and environmental experience, through deft artistry and place-specific aesthetics. Our attention will focus on the contexts from which particular Native American literatures emerge, the ethics to consider when entering Indigenous intellectual territory, and close attention to common themes and techniques that frequently appear in contemporary Native American literature. Readings will feature a range of novels, poetry, short fiction, graphic novel/comics, and film.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AG, D-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(CU-SBY)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2825, "crseId": 370534, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "3324", "titleShort": "Cayuga Language and Culture", "titleLong": "Cayuga Language and Culture", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 2941, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:40PM", "timeEnd": "04:35PM", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/05/2022", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 6, "netid": "jlm556", "firstName": "Jessica", "middleName": "Lynn", "lastName": "Martin"}], "pattern": "F", "facilityDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall G24", "bldgDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "GSHG24", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/17/2022", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "HY", "instrModeDescrshort": "HY-OLIP", "instrModeDescr": "Hybrid-Online and In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "LING", "catalogNbr": "3324", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "LING", "catalogNbr": "6324", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/22/2022", "sessionEndDt": "12/17/2022", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "An introduction to the language and culture of the Cayuga (Gayogo\u0331h\u00f3\ua789n\u01eb\u02bc) people. Basic language instruction provided in an immersive learning environment, focusing on the relationship of language and culture to plants and growing.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "This course does not\u00a0count toward the A&amp;S language requirement.\u00a0 Neither does the course count towards the Linguistics major or minor without an additional 1 credit Independent Study course.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2825, "crseId": 361756, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "3330", "titleShort": "IndigenousPlace-BasedEcolKnowl", "titleLong": "Ways of Knowing: Indigenous and Place-Based Ecological Knowledge", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 16649, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "09:40AM", "timeEnd": "10:55AM", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/05/2022", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ksk28", "firstName": "Karim-Aly", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Kassam"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Fernow Hall G24", "bldgDescr": "Fernow Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "FRNG24", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/17/2022", "addConsent": "D", "addConsentDescr": "Department Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "3330", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "NTRES", "catalogNbr": "3330", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "NTRES", "catalogNbr": "6330", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/22/2022", "sessionEndDt": "12/17/2022", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Based on indigenous and place-based \"ways of knowing,\" this course (1) presents a theoretical and humanistic framework from which to understand generation of ecological knowledge; (2) examines processes by which to engage indigenous and place-based knowledge of natural resources, the nonhuman environment, and human-environment interactions; and (3) reflects upon the relevance of this knowledge to climatic change, resource extraction, food sovereignty, medicinal plant biodiversity, and issues of sustainability and conservation. The fundamental premise of this course is that human beings are embedded in their ecological systems.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AG, D-AG, KCM-AG, SBA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(CU-CEL, CU-ITL, CU-SBY)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "Interested graduate students should enroll in the graduate version of the course, NTRES 6330.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: juniors and seniors.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["To appreciate natural resource development from a human ecological perspective;", "To apply the interdisciplinary lens of human ecology to understand human and environmental relations;", "To appreciate the complex interconnectivity between the ecological and the cultural;", "To comprehend that individual actions informed by cultural systems manifest themselves in social structures that rely on ecological foundations;", "To extend the notion of transdisciplinary to include indigenous and place-based knowledge;", "To situate indigenous and local knowledge within a humanistic framework of knowledge generation;", "To illustrate the participatory and experiential basis of indigenous and place-based knowledge;", "To propose a method best suited for researching such knowledge processes; and", "To value the contributions of indigenous and place-based knowledge in the context of socio-cultural and environmental change and natural resource utilization."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2825, "crseId": 365706, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "3560", "titleShort": "Indigenous Philosophies", "titleLong": "Thinking from a Different Place: Indigenous Philosophies", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 17875, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:45PM", "timeEnd": "04:00PM", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/05/2022", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "etc7", "firstName": "Eric", "middleName": "T.", "lastName": "Cheyfitz"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Caldwell Hall 400", "bldgDescr": "Caldwell Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "CLD400", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/17/2022", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "C", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "3562", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "3560", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/22/2022", "sessionEndDt": "12/17/2022", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "The Western nation-state has failed to solve the two most pressing, indeed catastrophic, global problems: poverty and climate change. This failure is due to the inability of national policy to imagine a world beyond a boundary drawn by the formative capitalist ideas of property, production, and profit. The course will begin by discussing the historical origin and continuing force of these ideas while raising questions about their limits. Then it will look at a range of alternative ideas about how the world should work if we want to keep it socially, economically, and ecologically in balance. The alternatives we will query come from a range of Indigenous writers of fiction, poetry, and theory, who locate themselves in Native American (north and south), Aboriginal, and Maori communities.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(D-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(CU-SBY)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Explain Indigenous theory and practice in dealing with social, economic, and environmental issues.", "Contrast Indigenous theory and practice with Western approaches to these issues at a time of gross income inequality and environmental collapse worldwide.", "Think critically about the most effective ways to deal with these global issues after having considered both approaches to these issues."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2825, "crseId": 368686, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "4200", "titleShort": "Philosophies of Dispossession", "titleLong": "Locke and the Philosophies of Dispossession: Indigenous America's Interruptions and Resistances", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 17472, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:30PM", "timeEnd": "04:30PM", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/05/2022", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "tar37", "firstName": "Troy", "middleName": "A.", "lastName": "Richardson"}], "pattern": "M", "facilityDescr": "Caldwell Hall 400", "bldgDescr": "Caldwell Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "CLD400", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/17/2022", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "6200", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "4220", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "6220", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "PHIL", "catalogNbr": "4941", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "PHIL", "catalogNbr": "6941", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/22/2022", "sessionEndDt": "12/17/2022", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This course looks at the philosopher John Locke as a philosopher of dispossession. There is a uniquely Lockean mode of missionization, conception of mind and re-formulations of the 'soul' applied to dispossess Indigenous peoples of the social institutions, intellectual traditions and the material bases and practices which sustain(ed) them. While colonization is typically used as a kind of shorthand for this process, we will be attempting to stay focused on the specific dimensions of Lockean dispossession and its mutually informing relationship with English colonialism.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "Recommended prerequisite: AIIS 1110\u00a0and AIIS 4000/AIIS 6000.", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Demonstrate a fluency in the philosophical formulations for settler colonialism and the historical and ongoing dispossessing of Native Americans\u2014specifically, a fluency in Locke's philosophies of 1) the workmanship theory of property, 2) of consciousness and the modern \"self,\" 3) theories of mind, 4) metaphysics and theology.", "Develop enhanced interpretive abilities through formal presentations and writing assignments.", "Apply pedagogical skills in teaching course content where they lead seminar topics.", "Employ sharpened interpretation and critical analysis skills through course writing assignments and structured editorial assistance to 1) concisely convey central argument(s) of texts, 2) make warrantable claims using relevant historical, philosophical, legal and material/empirical evidence, 3) clearly indicate one's positionality in developing arguments."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2825, "crseId": 366149, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "4625", "titleShort": "Contemp Native Amer Fiction", "titleLong": "Contemporary Native American Fiction", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 17878, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "09:40AM", "timeEnd": "10:55AM", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/05/2022", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "etc7", "firstName": "Eric", "middleName": "T.", "lastName": "Cheyfitz"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Caldwell Hall 400", "bldgDescr": "Caldwell Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "CLD400", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/17/2022", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "4627", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "4625", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/22/2022", "sessionEndDt": "12/17/2022", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "If you haven't read contemporary U.S. American Indian fiction, then it might be fair to ask how much you know about the United States, its origins and its current condition. Since the 1960s, American Indians have been producing a significant body of award-wining novels and short stories. In 1969, for example, N. Scott Momaday, from the Kiowa nation, won the Pulitzer Prize for his novel House Made of Dawn and in 2012 Louise Erdrich, who is Anishinaabe, won the National Book Award for her novel The Round House. In between these two notable moments and since we can list an impressive number of Native storytellers whose work is aesthetically powerful, offering us a narrative of the United States that counters the official history. Centrally the course will focus on the various formal approaches Native writers take from surrealism to realism in representing the (post)colonial situation of Indian country and the ongoing resistance in Indian country to the U.S. legal and political regime.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Identify contemporary Indigenous writer and the genders they are writing in.", "Communicate the main theme in the discussed novels that relate to contemporary and historical issues in Indigenous communities, such as land rights, child welfare, protection of Indigenous women, Indigenous governance systems, construction of racial and colonial regimes, etc.", "Analyze historical and legal trends in Indigenous-federal relations."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2825, "crseId": 354481, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AIIS", "catalogNbr": "4970", "titleShort": "Independent Study", "titleLong": "Independent Study", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "IND", "ssrComponentLong": "Independent Study", "section": "601", "classNbr": 14268, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "", "timeEnd": "", "startDt": "08/22/2022", "endDt": "12/05/2022", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 3, "netid": "jwp35", "firstName": "Jon", "middleName": "W.", "lastName": "Parmenter"}], "pattern": "", "facilityDescr": "To Be Assigned", "bldgDescr": null, "facilityDescrshort": "TBA", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "Prerequisites: Permission of instructor required. Students must register using the CALS Special Studies form (https://cals